1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,879
CRUD honesty fails on the quay.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,615
A row named berths with columns status and
vessel_id does not explain why two
schedulers cannot both reserve B7 for
overlapping windows,

3
00:00:11,735 --> 00:00:23,135
or why billing says “reserved” while the
yard says “empty.” Domain-driven design
starts with ubiquitous language and
bounded contexts — then protects
invariants inside aggregates like Berth.

4
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:37,560
In the scheduling context, people say
*assign*, *reserve*, *release*,
*conflict*, *tidal window*. In billing,
the same physical quay might be *billable
reservation* and *demurrage start*. Do not
force one God model.

5
00:00:37,679 --> 00:00:44,832
Bounded contexts allow different models
that integrate deliberately — often
through events or anti-corruption
adapters.

6
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:58,871
When a ticket says “fix berth status,” ask
which language the speaker is using before
you touch a column. Look at this java for
a moment. Look at this java for a moment.
The aggregate root is Berth.

7
00:00:58,991 --> 00:01:12,911
Outside code does not sprinkle
reservations.add on a list from a
controller — it calls reserve so overlaps
stay impossible to forget. Repositories
load and save aggregates, not arbitrary
rows for every join the UI wants.

8
00:01:13,031 --> 00:01:27,408
Domain events like BerthReserved speak the
language of the context and become
integration points later. Walk a
concurrency failure. Two schedulers load
B7, both see no overlap, both call
reserve, both save.

9
00:01:27,527 --> 00:01:37,967
Without optimistic locking (@Version on
the aggregate) or a DB exclusion
constraint on windows, you get a double
booking that the domain method alone
cannot see across transactions.

10
00:01:38,087 --> 00:01:46,151
DDD does not replace transactional
discipline; it concentrates the rules
where they belong and still needs
persistence concurrency control.

11
00:01:46,271 --> 00:02:00,767
Symptom in ops: two vessels told they own
B7; yard crane chaos; billing double-
charges. DDD is not mandatory ceremony for
every screen. A simple reference-data
editor for hazard codes may stay CRUD.

12
00:02:00,887 --> 00:02:09,816
Use aggregates where invariants hurt when
violated — berth conflicts, gate release
against unpaid invoices, tally counts that
must match the manifest.

13
00:02:09,935 --> 00:02:18,935
Value objects (BerthId, ImoNumber, Money)
kill primitive obsession — a method that
accepts three Strings will swap gate id
and IMO eventually.

14
00:02:19,055 --> 00:02:30,839
Failure symptoms of anemic models:
BerthManager services with all the ifs,
entities that are bags of getters, and
every new rule added in a controller “just
this once.” Opposite ceremony:

15
00:02:30,959 --> 00:02:39,983
aggregates spanning half the quay graph so
every reservation loads the world — keep
aggregate boundaries tight around
consistency needs, not around the ER
diagram.

16
00:02:40,103 --> 00:02:49,055
Trade-offs: richer models cost design time
and careful mapping to ORM. They pay off
when rules change weekly and bugs are
expensive.

17
00:02:49,175 --> 00:02:52,487
Ubiquitous language meetings sound soft;

18
00:02:52,607 --> 00:03:04,343
they prevent scheduling and billing from
shipping incompatible meanings of
“reserved.” A misconception is equating
DDD with microservices — you can have rich
aggregates in a modular monolith.

19
00:03:04,463 --> 00:03:17,831
Another is anemic “entities” that are only
getters/setters with all rules in services
named *Manager. A third is one enterprise-
wide entity model shared by scheduling and
billing until every change requires a
committee.

20
00:03:17,951 --> 00:03:29,807
Once BerthReserved is a fact inside the
process, the next pressure is letting
billing react without a synchronous call
from scheduling on every assignment. That
shift is event-driven architecture.
